---
// The one page on this site aimed squarely at the thing competitors cannot
// honestly claim.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS: every PDF tool page here used to target the head term —
// "merge pdf", "split pdf" — which is ground iLovePDF, Smallpdf and Adobe
// have owned for a decade. A new domain does not take that by frontal
// assault. But "merge pdf without uploading" and its relatives are queries
// those sites structurally cannot answer, because their whole architecture
// depends on receiving the file. Low volume, genuinely winnable.
//
// WHY IT IS FAIR TO COMPETITORS: comparison pages that only flatter the
// author get ignored. The section naming what upload-based services do
// better is not modesty — it is the thing that makes the rest believable,
// and it is also simply true.

import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { publishedTools } from '../data/site.js';

const pdfTools = publishedTools.filter((t) => t.category === 'pdf');

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is it actually safe to use online PDF tools?',
    a: 'It depends entirely on whether the tool uploads your file. Reputable services encrypt transfers and delete files after a few hours, and for a holiday itinerary that is fine. For an employment contract, a medical letter, a passport scan or anything under NDA, the safer answer is a tool that never receives the file at all.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I know a tool isn’t uploading my file?',
    a: 'Turn off your wifi and try it. A tool that works with no connection cannot be sending your document anywhere. That is the whole test, and it takes ten seconds.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do most PDF sites upload files then?',
    a: 'Historically browsers could not do this work. Processing a PDF meant a server. Modern browsers can, but rebuilding an established service around that is a large change with no obvious commercial upside — a server-based tool is easier to meter, gate behind an account, and charge for.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a file size limit?',
    a: 'There is no upload cap because there is no upload. The limit is your own device’s memory. On a laptop a few hundred pages is comfortable; on an older phone rather less. If a very large file struggles, that is the constraint you are hitting.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does this work offline?',
    a: 'Yes. Load the page once, and the PDF tools keep working with no connection. You can add them to your phone’s home screen and use them on a plane.',
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="PDF Tools That Don't Upload Your Files | gazza.ltd"
  description="Merge, split and edit PDFs without uploading them anywhere. Everything runs in your browser, so confidential documents never leave your device. Free, no sign-up."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/pdf">PDF Tools</a> › <span>Without uploading</span>
    </nav>

    <h1>PDF tools that don't upload your files</h1>
    <p class="standfirst">
      Most online PDF tools work by receiving your document, processing it on
      their servers, and promising to delete it later. These ones never receive
      it at all.
    </p>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What normally happens to your file</h2>
      <p>
        When you drop a PDF onto a typical online tool, the file is transferred
        to that company's servers, processed there, and held for a period before
        deletion — commonly a few hours. The established services are
        transparent about this and take it seriously: transfers are encrypted,
        retention windows are short, and deletion is automated.
      </p>
      <p>
        For most documents that is a perfectly sensible trade. You get powerful
        processing for free and the file is gone by teatime.
      </p>
      <p>
        But it is still a trade, and for some documents it is the wrong one. An
        employment contract, a payslip, a medical letter, a passport scan, a
        client file under NDA, anything covered by a confidentiality clause —
        for these, "we delete it afterwards" is a promise you cannot verify,
        made by a company you have no relationship with.
      </p>

      <h2>How to tell whether a tool uploads your file</h2>
      <p>
        You do not have to take anyone's word for it, including ours. There is a
        test that takes ten seconds and cannot be faked:
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Load the page, turn off your wifi, then use the tool.</strong>
        If it still works, your file is not going anywhere — because it
        physically cannot. If it fails or hangs, the work was happening on
        somebody else's computer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Try it here. Open <a href="/merge-pdf">the merge tool</a>, switch on
        flight mode, and combine two files. It will work. That is not a claim
        about our intentions; it is a fact about where the processing happens.
      </p>

      <h2>Where upload-based tools are genuinely better</h2>
      <p>
        Being straight about this, because it matters if you are choosing:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>OCR and text recognition.</strong> Turning a scanned image
          into searchable text needs serious processing, and server-based tools
          do it far better than anything running in a browser tab.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Heavy compression.</strong> Squeezing a large PDF down while
          keeping it readable benefits from the same horsepower.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Very large files.</strong> A server has more memory than your
          phone. If you are working with a 500-page scanned document, an upload
          service will cope where a browser may not.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Conversion between formats.</strong> PDF to Word, Excel and
          similar are hard problems that are still better solved server-side.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you need those, use a service that does them well. The point is not
        that uploading is wrong — it is that for merging, splitting, rotating
        and reordering, uploading is unnecessary, and unnecessary risk is worth
        removing.
      </p>

      <h2>What runs entirely on your device</h2>
    </div>

    <ul class="card-grid">
      {pdfTools.map((t) => (
        <li>
          <a class="card" href={`/${t.slug}`}>
            <span class="name">{t.title}</span>
            <span class="desc">{t.metaDescription.slice(0, 80)}…</span>
          </a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        All free, no sign-up, no account, no watermark and no file limit beyond
        your own device's memory. They also work offline — you can
        <a href="/pdf">add them to your home screen</a> and use them with no
        connection at all.
      </p>

      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      {faq.map((f) => (
        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>{f.q}</h3>
          <p set:html={f.a} />
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>

  <script
    type="application/ld+json"
    set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: f.q,
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
      })),
    })}
  />
</Base>
